[Seven essays](../README.md) · [Current selection](../../../docs/selection.md) · [After the midterm](../../../docs/post-midterm.md)

A researcher destroys a cheap source label that would help interpret an upcoming audit. Her later confidence can fit every piece of evidence she has left, even though keeping the label would have made the inquiry more accurate. The essay asks what evaluating evidence management adds to evaluating a belief's fit with present evidence.

[Original release R1](R1.md) · [Original draft](draft.md) · [Argument critique](critique-A.md) · [Literature critique](critique-B.md) · [Author response](author-response.md)

## The resulting argument

R1 defends a conditional distinction: proportionate use of retained evidence can coexist with a poor earlier decision about what to retain. The argument requires a separate reason to value the future accuracy of inquiry. It challenges the claim that fit with available evidence exhausts a method's merit, while leaving broader theories of rational action open.

